        difficulty: { level: 'Medium', text: "A crane is steel fabrication plus bought-in engines, castings and hydraulics. The skill lies in cost engineering, a service network across India and keeping up with emission rules (the jump to new CEV-V engines raised crane prices sharply last year). Heavy truck and crawler cranes are harder, and Chinese makers are strong there." },
        moat: "Scale, a nationwide dealer and service network and long leadership in pick-and-carry cranes let it push through about 10% of price rises in six months while volumes still grew 17%. The weak spot is heavy cranes, where Chinese imports compete and the Finance Ministry has not imposed the anti-dumping duty that the government's own investigators recommended.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Infrastructure and factory building**: roads, railways, high-speed rail, urban works, manufacturing, logistics, data centres, solar and oil-storage projects all need lifting. Demand was strong until May–June 2026, then slowed in the rains as it does every year.", "**The Hydra recovery**: small operators held back last year after the switch to new emission-norm engines raised Hydra prices steeply; that caution has faded over the past 7–8 months and Hydra volumes are rising again.", "**Price rises**: it raised prices in January, May and June 2026 (about 10% in all) to cover costlier steel, which also lifts revenue.", "**Defence and exports**: FY27 defence sales are expected above ₹200cr, a repeat order of over ₹100cr is hoped for, and a new Plant 9 for defence and special machines is due around end-2026. The KATO joint venture should add meaningful revenue from FY28.", "**Monsoon and acquisitions**: a weak monsoon can move sales 5–10% in the second half, mostly in smaller towns. Management names acquisitions and exports as its two biggest future growth drivers."],
